  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nAn area of light rain has overspread the western half of the\nQuad State so far today, and it should break up, if not\ncompletely dissipate by the end of the day as it attempts to\npush through the eastern half of the region. A few locations may\nreceive a few hundredths before it gives up.\n\nAnother upper-level disturbance will bring scattered to numerous\nshowers and possibly a few thunderstorms southeast across the\nQuad State tonight into Saturday. The best chances will be late\ntonight and Saturday morning. Much of the most recent guidance\nkeeps the Evansville Tri State dry, but confidence in said\nguidance is not that high. Instability will be virtually non-\nexistent across the region tonight and Saturday morning, so thunder\nseems like quite a stretch at this time.\n\nThe 12Z HREF mean has surface-based CAPE around 2500J/kg over\nsoutheast Missouri Saturday afternoon, so a few thunderstorms \nmay develop over there behind the better overall chances of rain\nearlier in the day. Shear will be lacking, but not non-existent,\nso a stray strong storm with gusty winds cannot be ruled out.\n\nA Heat Advisory has been issued for the western half of the\nregion for Sunday through Tuesday, as an upper-level ridge\nbuilds eastward over the region. Based on this NBM forecast, the\nentire region will need at least an Advisory Monday and most\nlikely again on Tuesday. Beginning an Advisory in the 4th \nperiod is about as far as we wanted to push it at this point, \nso we just settled for the western half for now, and will let \nfuture shifts expand as they see fit for Monday and Tuesday.\n\nThere is some concern for convection riding south along the \nedge of the warmer air aloft, which could impact the Tri State \nand Pennyrile Sunday night and again Monday night. Of course, \nthis could potentially hold back heat indices from the currently\ndry NBM forecast. Also, this type of set up can lead to some\npretty heavy rainfall. Any significant low-level jet seems to\nhold to the north of the Quad State for now, but if that were to\nchange a heavy rainfall and flash flooding threat could develop\nat some point Sunday night through Monday night. For now there\nis not enough consensus in the guidance for there to even be a\nconsistent 15+% PoP, so we will have to settle for just\nmentioning the possibility at this time.\n\nA cold front is expected to pass southward through the Quad\nState Tuesday afternoon and evening. It would likely not be\nstrong enough to thwart the heat and humidity build up\nthroughout the region Tuesday, but it is another thing to\nmonitor. However, by Wednesday, temperatures will trend back \nnear normal and stay there for the remainder of the work week. \nThe NBM struggles to have any mentionable PoP until a 20% chance\nshows up on Friday. However, with tangible northwest flow and \nlingering, albeit not extreme, heat and humidity, A round of \nconvection at some point would not be shocking over the second\nhalf of the week.",
